<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pierre Laval</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1883">1883</a>–<a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pierre Laval was a French politician who served as prime minister in the 1930s and became the leading figure of collaboration in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vichy">Vichy</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. As head of government from 1942 to 1944 he worked closely with Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, sending French workers to the Reich and assisting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportation</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>. After the Liberation he was tried for treason and executed in October 1945.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pierre-laval.jpg" alt="Pierre Laval" width="320" height="415" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Agence de presse Meurisse · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pierre_Laval_a_Meurisse_1931.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Pierre Jean Marie Laval was born on 28 June 1883 in Châteldon, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vichy">Vichy</a> in Auvergne, the son of Gilbert Laval and Claudine Tournaire. His father worked as a café proprietor and postman, and the family was comfortably off compared with the rest of the village. Laval abandoned natural science studies for law and in 1909 became a lawyer who defended strikers, trade unionists and left-wing agitators against government prosecution. In 1914 he was elected to the Chamber of Deputies as a Socialist for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seine">Seine</a>, at nearly 31 the youngest member of the Chamber, and during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> he held to pacifist convictions.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After losing his seat in 1919 Laval left the Socialist party, was elected mayor of Aubervilliers in 1923, and returned to the Chamber as an independent in 1924. He was Minister of Public Works in 1925, held other posts under Aristide Briand, and later served as Minister of Labour. Between 1927 and 1930 he accumulated a sizeable personal fortune, which after the war led to charges that he had used his political position to enrich himself.</p><p>He formed his first government on 27 January 1931. During the Austrian banking crisis of that year he blocked a proposed international loan for nationalist reasons and demanded diplomatic concessions from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, and the Creditanstalt declared bankruptcy on 11 May 1931. He joined Gaston Doumergue's government in 1934, became Foreign Minister after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-barthou">Louis Barthou</a> was assassinated in October, and returned as prime minister in 1935. His handling of the Abyssinia Crisis was widely denounced as appeasement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Benito Mussolini</a> and prompted his resignation in 1936.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippe-petain">Philippe Pétain</a>'s Vichy government Laval was vice-president of the Council of Ministers from July to December 1940. He openly sympathised with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazism">National Socialism</a>, was convinced that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> would win the war, and worked to dismantle the Third Republic. Pétain dismissed him on 13 December 1940, citing an incompatibility of working styles, and had him arrested and driven to his home. Laval returned to power in April 1942. In a radio speech on 22 June 1942 he said he wished for a German victory, since otherwise Bolshevism would establish itself everywhere, and the speech made plain to many that the Vichy government was subservient to Germany.</p><p>After the Liberation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1944 the Germans arrested him and moved him with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vichy">Vichy</a> remnant to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigmaringen">Sigmaringen</a>. He fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in April 1945, was expelled and arrested by the Free French, and was imprisoned at Fresnes. His trial, which scholars such as Robert Paxton and Geoffrey Warner say showed the poisonous atmosphere of the purge trials, ended in a death sentence for plotting against the security of the state and collaboration. Laval was executed by firing squad on 15 October 1945.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pierre Laval” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 10 July 1940 the Chamber of Deputies and the Senate, lobbied with promises and threats by <b>Pierre Laval</b> and Raphaël Alibert, voted by 569 to 80 to give full powers to Pétain.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vichy-france">Vichy France · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Châteldon, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Fresnes Prison, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.98,3.52&amp;year=1923" aria-label="Châteldon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4633 544 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Pierre Laval"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5083 814h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5083 814h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.98,3.52&amp;year=1923">What was here in 1923? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
